{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" -}}
#!/bin/bash
#
# macOS defaults that make the desktop feel responsive rather than floaty.
# Everything here is a per-user `defaults` key -- no sudo, nothing system-wide,
# and every one is reversible with `defaults delete`.
#
set -e

echo "Applying macOS defaults..."

# --- Spaces / Mission Control ----------------------------------------------
# The slide animation when switching desktops. Default is 0.2s, which reads as
# floaty when you swipe between Spaces constantly. 0.1 keeps the animation
# (so you still see which direction you moved) at half the duration.
#   revert:  defaults delete com.apple.dock expose-animation-duration
defaults write com.apple.dock expose-animation-duration -float 0.1

# How long a drag must hover at the screen edge before it flips to the next
# Space. Default is ~0.75s.
#   revert:  defaults delete com.apple.dock workspaces-edge-delay
defaults write com.apple.dock workspaces-edge-delay -float 0.05

# Do not reorder Spaces by recent use -- yabai and the SketchyBar Space
# indicators both assume Space N stays Space N.
defaults write com.apple.dock mru-spaces -bool false

# --- Window behaviour -------------------------------------------------------
# Instant window resize for AppKit windows.
defaults write NSGlobalDomain NSWindowResizeTime -float 0.001

killall Dock 2>/dev/null || true

cat <<'NOTICE'
macOS defaults applied.

If switching Spaces still feels too floaty, the stronger option is Reduce
Motion, which replaces the slide with a near-instant crossfade:

  System Settings > Accessibility > Display > Reduce Motion

That is left off by default here because it also flattens animations
everywhere else in the system.
NOTICE
{{- end -}}
